<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cloistered-rule">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cloistered rule</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1086">1086</a>–<a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Insei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cloistered rule, or insei, was a form of government in Heian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in which an emperor abdicated but kept power, while his chosen successor performed the ceremonial duties of the monarchy. As a system it usually refers to the practice begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shirakawa">Emperor Shirakawa</a>, who abdicated in 1086 in favour of his four-year-old son, and followed by his successors until the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> in 1192. The retired emperors, some of whom entered Buddhist monastic life, acted to counterbalance the Fujiwara regents and the warrior class, and kept courts and troops of their own. Their military units helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-clan">Taira clan</a> rise to power, and the system lingered formally long after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokyu-war">Jōkyū War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cloistered-rule.jpg" alt="Cloistered rule" width="330" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 高階隆兼（模写：板橋貫雄） · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:白河上皇の御幸.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ritsuryo system allowed retired emperors some limited powers, and early examples include Empress Jitō, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-uda">Emperor Uda</a> in the 7th, 8th and 9th centuries. By the end of the 10th century the Hokke family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a> held power through the office of Imperial Regent, and the emperor was increasingly a figurehead. In 1068 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-sanjo">Emperor Go-Sanjō</a> became the first emperor in almost 200 years unrelated to the Hokke family by marriage or blood.</p><p>While the Fujiwara were occupied with internal conflicts he issued several laws, most notably the Enkyū Shōen Regulation Decree, and so weakened the regency. In 1072 he fell ill and abdicated in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shirakawa">Emperor Shirakawa</a>, and died the following year, too soon to exert power after his abdication, but he had paved the way for cloistered rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Retired emperors were called Daijō Tennō or Jōkō, and one who entered a Buddhist monastic community became a Cloistered Emperor, Daijō Hōō. In 1086 Shirakawa abdicated in favour of his four-year-old son, Emperor Horikawa. His aim appeared to be to protect his son from his younger brother, who threatened to become a pretender, but after his retirement he used his own power to set the system in motion.</p><p>Separate imperial courts, the In no Chō, grew up round the retired emperors, and their will was carried out through documents called Inzen and In no Chō Kudashi Bumi. The cloistered emperors also had their own troops, the Hokumen no Bushi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>These military units eventually led to the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-clan">Taira clan</a>, who used their membership to gather political and economic power. The end of the Heian period saw a rapid succession of cloistered emperors, so that several retired emperors lived at once. The Hōgen Rebellion, after the death of Emperor Toba, was a direct clash between an emperor and an emperor emeritus. The end of the reign of Emperor Go-Shirakawa was marked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shōgun.</p><p>The system was not abandoned at once. When Go-Toba, a grandson of Go-Shirakawa, tried to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate, his forces were defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokyu-war">Jōkyū War</a>, and the shogunate then reduced the power and finances of the retired emperors. Cloistered rule continued formally for another 200 years, a retired emperor presiding as head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> court with the approval of the shogunate. The last person to use the title Daijō Hōō was Emperor Reigen, in 1687.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cloistered rule” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
